# Mixxxxx Code Assessment, 2026-07-26

Method: read the actual source tree and build artifacts. Every claim below cites a
file and line. Nothing here is taken from PRD.md or README.md, both of which are
running ahead of the code.

Reviewed commit state: `build/mixxx.exe` and `build/mixxx-test.exe` both linked
2026-07-26, same timestamp. Symbols present (`mixxx.pdb`, 98 MB).

## 1. Build configuration (verified, `build/CMakeCache.txt`)

| Option | Value | Consequence |
|---|---|---|
| `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` | RelWithDebInfo | debuggable release |
| `BUILD_TESTING` | ON | `mixxx-test.exe` is built and current |
| `FFMPEG` | ON | video module compiled in |
| `ENGINEPRIME` | ON | libdjinterop linked |
| `ONNX_RUNTIME` | OFF | **stem separation is NOT in this binary** |
| `QML` | ON | QML skin path returns nullptr for VideoWidget, see 3.6 |

Video sources are attached to `mixxx-lib` (CMakeLists.txt:3638-3643), not to the
`mixxx` executable target. That matters: `mixxx-test.exe` links `mixxx-lib`, so
the video and export code is unit-testable today with no build changes.

## 2. What is genuinely real

These were traced call site by call site, not inferred from docs.

- **FFmpeg decode.** `VideoDecoder` (QThread) does real `avformat_open_input` /
  `av_read_frame` / `avcodec_send_packet` / `avcodec_receive_frame`. Not a stub.
- **Companion autoload.** `VideoWidget::findCompanionVideo` resolves basename plus
  `.mp4` / `.mkv` / `.mov` / `.webm` in priority order. Real.
- **Skin integration.** `LegacySkinParser` handles `<VideoWidget>` (line 614) and
  `<PhaseIndicator>` (line 610). Both node types are actually emitted by skin XML:
  `decks/spinny_coverart.xml` (per deck), `decks/row_4_overviewSpinny.xml` templates
  in `phase_indicator.xml`, `skin.xml:437` templates in `video_output.xml`.
- **A/V sync.** `VideoSyncControl` is a real `EngineControl`, constructed inside
  `EngineBuffer` (`enginebuffer.cpp:238`) and `addControl()`-ed, so it runs on the
  per-buffer engine callback. It publishes true engine position in seconds to
  `[ChannelN],video_audio_clock`. `VideoDecoder::run()` reads that CO each loop and
  `decodePacket()` implements the documented three-tier drift response
  (hard seek above 2s, frame skip above 3 frame durations, msleep throttle when
  ahead). This claim in the PRD holds up.
- **Export controls registration.** `registerExportControls()` is called from
  `coreservices.cpp:668`, and `ExportController` really does `connect()` all seven
  push buttons to exporter instantiations. The Sprint 4 "unwired" note is stale.
- **Exporter classes.** `RekordboxExporter` (289 lines), `SeratoExporter` (17.8 KB),
  `VirtualDjExporter` (10 KB) are complete implementations, not stubs.

## 3. What is broken

### 3.1 VideoMixer cannot mix (CRITICAL, this is the fork's reason to exist)

`videodecoder.cpp:74` and `videodecoder.cpp:250`:

```cpp
VideoMixer::instance().registerDecoder(m_videoStreamIndex, this);
VideoMixer::instance().pushFrame(m_videoStreamIndex, image, m_lastPts);
```

`m_videoStreamIndex` is set at `videodecoder.cpp:33` from
`av_find_best_stream(m_formatCtx, AVMEDIA_TYPE_VIDEO, ...)`. That is the index of
the video stream **within the container**, not the deck number. For a standard MP4
it is 0 for every deck.

Consequence: all decks collide on `m_frames[0]`. `VideoMixer::blendFrame()` builds
`activeDecks`, gets size 1 every time, takes the single-deck early branch, and
returns whichever deck pushed a frame most recently. The two-deck composite path at
`videomixer.cpp:64-75` is unreachable.

`VideoDecoder` already carries `m_group` (set by `VideoWidget::slotVideoEnabled`).
Deriving the deck index from it is the fix.

### 3.2 Blend curve is inverted

`videomixer.cpp:69`:

```cpp
double blend = fabs(crossfader); // 0 at center, 1 at edges
```

Deck B opacity is 0 at crossfader center and 1 at both extremes. A crossfader wants
the opposite at center, and asymmetric behaviour at the two ends. Also `double pos`
is computed at line 68 and never used, which the compiler should be warning about.

### 3.3 Dead controls presented as working

- `applySaturation()` is defined (`videomixer.cpp:103`) and declared
  (`videomixer.h:29`). Grep of all 1887 source files: **zero call sites**. The
  `[ChannelN],video_saturation` CO is created in `VideoWidget`'s constructor,
  documented in README and PRD as "Working", and does nothing.
- `VideoDecoder::setSpeed()` likewise: defined, declared, never called.

### 3.4 Pixel format hardcoded

`videodecoder.cpp:65`:

```cpp
m_swsCtx = sws_getContext(m_width, m_height, AV_PIX_FMT_YUV420P, ...)
```

Source format should be `m_codecCtx->pix_fmt`. Any 422p, 444p, 10-bit, or NV12
source will render as colour garbage.

### 3.5 Hardware decode does not engage

`initHardwareDecoder()` calls `av_hwdevice_ctx_create` and assigns
`m_codecCtx->hw_device_ctx`, but never installs a `get_format` callback on the codec
context. Without that, FFmpeg will not select the hardware pixel format and falls
back to software decode. The README and PRD both claim working D3D11VA and CUDA
decode. In practice it is software, which is the only reason 3.4 has not been
noticed: software decode of H.264 yields yuv420p, which happens to match the
hardcoded value.

### 3.6 QML skin disables video entirely

`legacyskinparser.cpp:1407-1410`: `parseVideoWidget` returns `nullptr` when
`CmdlineArgs::Instance().isQml()`. `QML=ON` in this build. Testing must use a legacy
skin (LateNight), not the QML skin.

### 3.7 Threading: QWaitCondition used without its mutex

`videodecoder.cpp:135`:

```cpp
if (!m_playing.loadRelaxed()) {
    m_cond.wait(&m_mutex, 100);
    continue;
}
```

`m_mutex` is not held at this point. `QWaitCondition::wait` requires the caller to
hold the mutex; it unlocks it internally. Unlocking a mutex the thread does not own
is undefined behavior. This path executes every time video is paused
(`video_enabled` set to 0 calls `VideoDecoder::pause()`).

### 3.8 Fullscreen window duplicates ControlObjects

`VideoWidget::slotVideoFullscreen` constructs `new VideoWidget(m_group, ...)`. That
constructor registers five ControlObjects (`video_enabled`, `video_fullscreen`,
`video_brightness`, `video_contrast`, `video_saturation`) on a group where they
already exist. Expect duplicate-key warnings and ambiguous signal delivery.

### 3.9 Export triggers pass wrong arguments (both paths always fail)

`export_controls.cpp:44-51` calls
`triggerRekordboxExport("[Channel1]", settingsPath, usbPath)`, which forwards to
`RekordboxExporter::exportTrack(const QString& trackPath, ...)`. That method runs
`WHERE track_locations.location = :path` against the group string. Zero rows, every
time. Emits "Track not found in library".

`export_controls.cpp:60` calls `ex->exportCrate(QString(), ...)`, an empty crate
name, against `SELECT id FROM crates WHERE name = ''`. Never matches.

Net: the buttons are now connected to a guaranteed failure rather than to nothing.
That is a regression in honesty terms, since PRD v0.4 marks this fixed.

### 3.10 The Rekordbox exporter does not write Rekordbox

`rekordboxexporter.cpp:146` calls `djinterop::engine::create_database(...,
e::latest_schema)`. libdjinterop supports only the Engine Library format (Denon
Engine DJ OS and Engine DJ Desktop, formerly Engine Prime). It does not write
Pioneer DeviceSQL `export.pdb`. Upstream tracking issues exist for Pioneer
OneLibrary support on both libdjinterop and mixxx; neither is resolved.

The code writes an Engine database into `usbPath/PIONEER/rekordbox`. That is the
wrong format for a CDJ and the wrong directory for Engine hardware, which expects
`Engine Library/`. Renaming the class and target directory makes the feature honest
and genuinely useful, since free Engine export is not widely available.

### 3.11 Hot cue enum filter is off by one

`cueinfo.h:12-14` defines `Invalid = 0, HotCue = 1, MainCue = 2`.
`rekordboxexporter.cpp:196` filters `if (cr.type == 2 && ...)` when collecting hot
cues, so it exports the main cue as a hot cue and discards every real hot cue.

### 3.12 exportCrate recreates the database per track

`exportCrate` loops over `exportTrack`, and `exportTrack` calls `create_database`
each time. Each track likely clobbers the previous one's database.

### 3.13 Export destination heuristic is unsafe

`defaultExportPath()` picks the first mounted volume that is valid, non-root, ready,
not NTFS, and under 128 GB. On a dev workstation that can select an SD card, a
mounted ISO, a FAT32 EFI partition, or a camera. It then `mkpath`s into it.

## 4. No OSC exists in this codebase

Full-tree grep of `src/` (1887 files scanned, 47 skipped) for `QUdpSocket`,
`OscServer`, `oscreceiver`, `11118`, `11119`: **zero hits**. The only `11118`
matches are float literals in `src/test/reference_buffers/`. The only OSC string in
the tree is upstream's `controllermappinginfo.cpp:81`, which logs "OSC product info
parsing not yet implemented".

The README's OSC Control Table and its "Configure Mixxx Preferences, MIDI/OSC"
instruction describe a feature that does not exist. Mixxx has no OSC preferences
page.

Downstream effect on `mixx-dj-mcp`:

- `osc_bridge.py` uses `SimpleUDPClient.send_message()`. UDP is fire and forget and
  never raises against a dead port.
- `OscBridge.is_connected()` returns `self._running`, which only reports whether its
  own listener thread started, not whether Mixxx is reachable.
- `tests/conftest.py` declares `auto_mock_bridge` with `autouse=True`, patching
  `get_bridge` across all five tool modules. The suite structurally cannot detect
  that nothing is listening.
- `scripts/osc_listener_test.py` listens on port 7000 and asserts
  `/composition/tempo` and `/layer1/opacity`. Those are Resolume addresses, not
  Mixxx ones.

Result: every `mixx_deck` and `mixx_mixer` tool reports success while doing nothing.

## 5. Test coverage of new code: zero

The `mixxx-test` source list (CMakeLists.txt:2130-2247) is the stock upstream set.
There is no `video*_test.cpp`, no `export*_test.cpp` beyond upstream's unrelated
`trackexport_test.cpp` (file-copy wizard), no `phasecontrol_test.cpp`, no
`videosynccontrol_test.cpp`.

Roughly 5000 lines added across video, export, phase, and sync. Zero tests added.
Every defect in section 3 would have been caught by a first test.

## 6. Summary judgment

The engine-level work is better than the doc-level work. `VideoSyncControl` is
correctly written and correctly integrated, which is the hardest part of this fork
and the part that took three sprints. The decode thread is real. The skin
integration is real.

The failures cluster in two places: identifier plumbing (stream index used as deck
index, group string used as file path, enum value off by one) and documentation
written from intent rather than from code. Both are cheap to fix. Neither requires
rearchitecting.
